    description "The Communards&#8217; Wall (French: Mur des F&#233;d&#233;r&#233;s) at the P&#232;re Lachaise cemetery is where 147 Commune soldiers along with another 19 officers were executed on May 28, 1871, during the Semaine sanglante, the suppression of the Paris Commune. The soldiers were buried in a common grave at the foot of the wall. The P&#232;re Lachaise cemetery was established in May 1804 on a land owned by the Jesuits for centuries, and where P&#232;re (&#34;Father&#34;) Lachaise, confessor of Louis XIV, lived the latter part of his life."
